steps.prompt:
Quantify recurring-revenue leakage from the live evidence map. Stay read-only.

Read the completed `evidence-map` output first and use only its verified schema, joins, window, and currency basis. If `subscription_analysis_available` is false, produce a precise setup/data-gap report and stop; do not substitute ordinary orders and call them subscriptions.

Use bounded SELECT-only `db_query` calls to calculate, when the fields exist:
- subscription count and normalized recurring value by lifecycle status; keep active, paused, past-due/payment-failed, cancelled, expired, and unknown statuses separate;
- new subscriptions, cancellations, pauses, resumptions/reactivations, skips, and failed/retried/paid renewal events within the chosen window;
- cancellation or failure timing by completed billing cycle or subscription age band, with an explicit `not available` when no reliable cycle/age evidence exists;
- recurring value currently exposed to involuntary failure separately from paused/cancelled value; do not label every non-active subscription recoverable;
- the top product or plan families represented in exposed recurring value when a defensible join exists.

Normalize mixed billing intervals to a monthly basis only when amount, interval unit, and interval count are all present. State the formula. Never add values across currencies; report separate currency blocks unless the data contains a documented base-currency conversion. Reconcile each headline to a status-level total and report the delta; a non-zero unexplained delta is a blocker, not rounding.

Do not mutate subscriptions, retry payments, attach discounts, contact customers, or create campaigns. Do not expose customer PII.

End with `STEP_OUTPUT:` containing `window`, `freshness`, `currency_blocks`, `status_summary`, `event_summary`, `cycle_or_age_cohorts`, `involuntary_exposure`, `paused_value`, `cancelled_value`, `plan_concentration`, `reconciliation`, `data_gaps`, and `assumptions`.

Measure how subscription acquisition and retention connect to reps, referrers, sponsors, or affiliates, using only the verified live join paths. Stay read-only.

Read the completed `evidence-map` output. If it marks rep/referral attribution unavailable, prove that no reliable path was found, return a graceful `attribution_available: false` result, and do not manufacture an attribution model.

When a verified path exists, use bounded SELECT-only `db_query` calls to calculate for the same window and currency rules as the lifecycle analysis:
- active subscription count and normalized recurring value attributed vs unattributed;
- new subscription count/value by credited rep or referral source;
- involuntary payment exposure and cancellations by credited rep or referral source;
- a minimum-volume quality view that prevents one tiny cohort from outranking a large cohort solely on a percentage; show numerator and denominator beside every rate;
- the highest-value rep-led intervention cohorts, identified by rep id plus display name only when available. Do not expose subscriber names or contact details.

Never assume sponsor, enroller, seller, buyer rep, affiliate, or referring rep mean the same thing. Name the exact attribution field used and label its business meaning `unverified` unless the schema or stored metadata defines it. Do not turn correlation into causation: differences between rep cohorts are signals for review, not proof a rep caused churn.

Reconcile attributed plus unattributed counts/value to the eligible population and report the delta. Do not edit rep credit, reassociate an order, send a message, or launch an incentive.

End with `STEP_OUTPUT:` containing `attribution_available`, `attribution_definition`, `window`, `freshness`, `coverage`, `rep_cohorts`, `unattributed`, `intervention_cohorts`, `reconciliation`, `data_gaps`, and `assumptions`.

Publish a CEO-ready Subscription Revenue Recovery dashboard from the verified recovery model, then write the copy block that can be used directly on a CEO value page.

Read the completed `recovery-model` output and preserve every needs-review finding, data gap, currency boundary, and scenario label. Call `list_dashboards` first. Then assemble one descriptor and call `show_dashboard`. The dashboard should be useful on a large screen and legible in a short demo:
- title `Subscription Revenue Recovery`; subtitle names the evidence window and says `Read-only analysis`; set `dataAsOf` to the actual evidence freshness timestamp;
- a headline `stat_tiles` row for active recurring value, involuntary exposure, exposed subscription count, and the 10% scenario, omitting any metric the evidence cannot support;
- one status-mix `donut_chart` or `stat_rows` card with raw numeric values and semantic format hints;
- one 5%/10%/20% `hero_chart` or `stat_rows` scenario card whose title includes `Scenario assumptions — not forecasts`;
- one rep-attribution leaderboard only when attribution is available, showing rep-level aggregates rather than subscriber PII; otherwise an `insight_banner` that states the verified attribution gap;
- one compact intervention table with eligible population, live value base, success metric, and owner;
- one assumptions/data-quality banner.

Pass raw numbers to the descriptor with semantic format hints; never pre-format numeric strings. The dashboard's `currency` and `currency_compact` formatters render a dollar sign, so use them only for values whose verified currency is USD. For every other currency, keep the metric numeric with `number` or `compact` and put the exact ISO currency code in the card title or metric label. Keep currencies in visibly separate cards/sections and do not publish an aggregate across them. If `show_dashboard` returns a validation error, repair the descriptor and call it again without changing the underlying metrics.

After the dashboard succeeds, render a `## CEO value page brief` in Markdown with exactly these sections: `Headline`, `What the live data shows`, `Modeled opportunity`, `Three actions`, `How we will measure`, `Assumptions and data gaps`, and `Approval boundary`. Keep it under 350 words. It must directly distinguish observed values from scenarios and state that no customers, subscriptions, payments, rep credits, messages, campaigns, or storefront content were changed.

Saving/showing this local dashboard is the only state change in this step. Do not mutate Fluid data and do not initiate an intervention.

End with `STEP_OUTPUT:` containing `dashboard_title`, `dashboard_id` when returned, `data_as_of`, `window`, `headline`, `observed_metrics`, `scenario_metrics`, `interventions`, `ceo_value_page_brief`, `assumptions`, `data_gaps`, and `fluid_writes_performed: false`.

Perform the final independent business-readiness review of the Subscription Revenue Recovery result. This is a read-only gate, not another analysis build.

Read the completed outputs and QA findings from `evidence-map`, `lifecycle-leakage`, `rep-attribution`, `recovery-model`, and `publish-dashboard`. Call `list_dashboards` to confirm the saved artifact and use the minimum bounded SELECT-only `db_query` checks needed to re-prove the headline's observed count/value and the eligible base used for its scenario.

Fail the gate when any of these is true: an observed number cannot be reproduced; currencies were combined without a documented conversion; modeled recovery reads like achieved or forecast revenue; a rep relationship was guessed; a needs-review finding was hidden; customer PII appears; the dashboard is missing; the CEO brief omits its approval boundary; or any Fluid write/action was claimed or performed. A complete no-data or partial-data result may pass only when the dashboard and CEO brief visibly say what is unavailable and use the count-based conservative headline.

Return a compact handoff for the person presenting this result:
- `CEO headline`: one sentence;
- `Live proof`: exact window, freshness, eligible count, observed exposure by currency, and source tables;
- `Modeled upside`: the 5%/10%/20% sensitivity table, explicitly assumptions;
- `Rep angle`: the verified attribution result or the exact gap;
- `Three actions`: each with metric and approval required;
- `Demo path`: three clicks or sentences covering dashboard -> biggest observed leak -> approval-safe action;
- `CEO page copy`: repeat the final under-350-word page brief verbatim;
- `Unresolved`: every data or QA limitation.

Do not mutate anything. End with `STEP_OUTPUT:` containing `ready: true|false`, `dashboard_id`, `ceo_headline`, `live_proof`, `modeled_upside`, `rep_angle`, `three_actions`, `demo_path`, `ceo_page_copy`, `approval_boundary`, and `unresolved`.
